            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the convex hull of a set of points - the smallest shape with no dents that contains them all, like a rubber band stretched round them (or, in 3-D, shrink-wrap) - and gives its size, the length of its boundary and which points lie on it.\n\nThe size of the hull is a measure of expansiveness: how much space a body's hands, feet and head span (the reach of a pose - its kinesphere), how much floor a group of performers covers, how wide a gesture's path is. Watching it over time shows a body opening and closing.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.convex_hull\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points: rows of x y (in 2-D) or x y z (in 3-D). Every input is measured and sent. Rows with nan are left out. Points all in a line (or, in 3-D, all in a plane) enclose nothing, and give 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsize:\nThe area enclosed (2-D) or the volume (3-D).\n\nboundary:\nThe length of the boundary (2-D) or the area of the surface (3-D).\n\nvertices:\nWhich points lie on the hull, by row number. Sent first, then boundary, then size.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a 2 x 2 square's corners and its centre. The hull is the square: area 4, perimeter 8; the centre, inside, is not one of the vertices (0 1 2 3).\n\nBottom: 12 random points in a 1 x 1 square, new every second - the area they span changes as they scatter.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.kdtree finds nearest points. np.ptp gives the extent along each axis - a box rather than a hull. scipy.label finds regions in a grid.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the convex hull of a set of points - the smallest shape with no dents that contains them all, like a rubber band stretched round them (or, in 3-D, shrink-wrap) - and gives its size, the length of its boundary and which points lie on it.\n\nThe size of the hull is a measure of expansiveness: how much space a body's hands, feet and head span (the reach of a pose - its kinesphere), how much floor a group of performers covers, how wide a gesture's path is. Watching it over time shows a body opening and closing.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.convex_hull\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points: rows of x y (in 2-D) or x y z (in 3-D). Every input is measured and sent. Rows with nan are left out. Points all in a line (or, in 3-D, all in a plane) enclose nothing, and give 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsize:\nThe area enclosed (2-D) or the volume (3-D).\n\nboundary:\nThe length of the boundary (2-D) or the area of the surface (3-D).\n\nvertices:\nWhich points lie on the hull, by row number. Sent first, then boundary, then size.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a 2 x 2 square's corners and its centre. The hull is the square: area 4, perimeter 8; the centre, inside, is not one of the vertices (0 1 2 3).\n\nBottom: 12 random points in a 1 x 1 square, new every second - the area they span changes as they scatter.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.kdtree finds nearest points. np.ptp gives the extent along each axis - a box rather than a hull. scipy.label finds regions in a grid."
        },
